IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tfa-dates`
IMPORT `tfa-rules`

-- Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) -- the questions of the MEMBER OF THE FAMILY.
--
-- SEAT: a member of the family of a person whose death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default -- the spouse,
-- partner, parent, child, sibling or other relative s 3 names -- for whose benefit the action is brought, and who brings
-- it in his or her own name where s 8 allows: "Am I a member of the family?", "Can an action be brought for my
-- benefit?", "Can I bring it myself, and from when?", "What must I give the defendant?", "Is my life insurance or super
-- taken off my damages?", "Can I get back the funeral and the headstone?", "How much of the amount recovered is mine?",
-- "If I refuse the money paid into court, who wins?". The executor or administrator, the defendant, an insurer, the
-- court and the jury get no seat: the court's and the jury's findings are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of tfa-rules.l4.
-- Two DEONTIC rules (the executor's 6 months, s 8(1); the particulars, s 7) are run inside the questions that need them;
-- no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- The time limit for the action is not in this Act: it is in the Limitation Act 1974 (Tas) ss 5A, 5B (encoded beside
-- this Act at subjects/au/tas/limitation-act-1974/encodings/baseline-2026-11/).
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas): the family member's questions`

§§ `Who may benefit`

@export Am I a "member of the family" of the person who died, so that an action for the death can be brought for my benefit (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 3)? Yes if you were their spouse, parent, stepparent, grandparent, child, stepchild, grandchild, brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister, or their partner in a personal relationship (under the Relationships Act 2003) that existed when the wrongful act, neglect or default happened. No for anyone else, such as an aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin or in-law.
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse; partner in a personal relationship; parent; stepparent; grandparent; child; stepchild; grandchild; brother; sister; half-brother; half-sister; or another relationship
      `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a partner: whether you were in a personal relationship (a significant relationship or caring relationship under the Relationships Act 2003) with the person at the time of the wrongful act, neglect or default that caused the death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a member of the family of the person who died` MEANS
    `s 3 -- I am a member of the family` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened`

@export Can an action for damages for the death be brought for my benefit (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) ss 3-6)? Yes if the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default that would have let the person who died sue for damages had they lived, you are a member of their family, and no action under the Act has already been brought for the death (only one action lies, and it is for the whole family). It does not matter that the death was a crime, and the Crown can be sued like anyone else (s 2). The time limit is in the Limitation Act 1974: usually 3 years from when the death and its cause were or ought to have been known.
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse; partner in a personal relationship; parent; stepparent; grandparent; child; stepchild; grandchild; brother; sister; half-brother; half-sister; or another relationship
      `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a partner: whether you were in a personal relationship (a significant relationship or caring relationship under the Relationships Act 2003) with the person at the time of the wrongful act, neglect or default that caused the death
      `was the death caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect or default (for example negligence), as a court would find it
      `could the person who died have sued had they lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, if the person had not died, they could have sued that person and recovered damages for the injury (for example, no defence would have defeated their own claim)
      `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 has already been brought for this death, by the executor or administrator or by any member of the family
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can an action be brought for my benefit for this death` MEANS
    `ss 4, 5, 6 -- an action lies for my benefit` (`s 3 -- I am a member of the family` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened`) (`s 4 -- the wrongdoer is liable to an action for the death` `was the death caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` `could the person who died have sued had they lived`) `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death`

§§ `Who brings the action`

@export From when can I bring the action for the death in my own name, instead of the executor or administrator (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) ss 5, 6, 8)? The action is brought by the executor or administrator of the person who died. If there is none, any member of the family may bring it now, for the whole family. If there is one, a member of the family may bring it only if the executor or administrator has not brought it within 6 months after the death: from the day after those 6 months end. Only one action lies for a death.
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse; partner in a personal relationship; parent; stepparent; grandparent; child; stepchild; grandchild; brother; sister; half-brother; half-sister; or another relationship
      `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a partner: whether you were in a personal relationship (a significant relationship or caring relationship under the Relationships Act 2003) with the person at the time of the wrongful act, neglect or default that caused the death
      `is there an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has an executor (named in a will, with probate) or an administrator (appointed by the court)
      `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 has already been brought for this death, by the executor or administrator or by any member of the family
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
GIVETH AN `Own-name position`
`From when can I bring the action in my own name` MEANS
    `s 8(1) -- when I may bring the action in my own name` (`s 3 -- I am a member of the family` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened`) `is there an executor or administrator` `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death` `the day of the death`

@export Can I bring the action for the death in my own name on this day (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) ss 5, 6, 8)? Yes if you are a member of the family, no action has already been brought for the death, and either there is no executor or administrator or more than 6 months have passed since the death without the executor or administrator bringing it. The action is then for the benefit of the whole family, as if the executor had brought it (s 8(2)).
GIVEN `what was my relationship to the person who died` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: spouse; partner in a personal relationship; parent; stepparent; grandparent; child; stepchild; grandchild; brother; sister; half-brother; half-sister; or another relationship
      `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a partner: whether you were in a personal relationship (a significant relationship or caring relationship under the Relationships Act 2003) with the person at the time of the wrongful act, neglect or default that caused the death
      `is there an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on the day you would bring the action, the person who died has an executor (named in a will, with probate) or an administrator (appointed by the court)
      `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 has already been brought for this death, by the executor or administrator or by any member of the family
      `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day I would bring it` IS A DATE @desc The day you would start the action (file the claim) in your own name
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I bring the action in my own name on this day` MEANS
    `s 8(1) -- I may bring the action in my own name on that day` (`s 3 -- I am a member of the family` `what was my relationship to the person who died` `was our personal relationship in existence when the wrongful act happened`) `is there an executor or administrator` `has an action under the Act already been brought for this death` `the day of the death` `the day I would bring it`

§§ `Bringing the action`
-- Arises only for a member of the family who is the plaintiff (brings the action in his or her own name).

@export As the plaintiff in an action for a death, have I given the defendant the particulars the Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 7 requires? Together with the statement of claim (the same day), you must deliver to the defendant or the defendant's Australian legal practitioner full particulars of every person for whom the action is brought and of the nature of the claim. The duty arises when you deliver the statement of claim.
GIVEN `have I delivered the statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have delivered your statement of claim to the defendant
      `the day I delivered the statement of claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you delivered the statement of claim (any date if you have not)
      `have I delivered particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have delivered particulars of the persons for whom the action is brought and of the nature of the claim
      `the day I delivered the particulars` IS A DATE @desc The day you delivered those particulars (any date if you have not)
      `who did I deliver them to` IS A `Delivered to` @desc Who received the particulars: the defendant; the defendant's Australian legal practitioner (lawyer); or someone else
      `do they name every person for whom the action is brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the particulars name every member of the family for whose benefit the action is brought
      `do they state the nature of the claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the particulars state the nature of the claim for which damages are sought (for example loss of financial support, funeral expenses)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Have I delivered the particulars section 7 requires` MEANS
    `s 7 -- I have delivered the particulars required` `have I delivered the statement of claim` `the day I delivered the statement of claim` `have I delivered particulars` `the day I delivered the particulars` `who did I deliver them to` `do they name every person for whom the action is brought` `do they state the nature of the claim`

§§ `Damages`

@export Can this payment or benefit that came to my family because of the death be taken off our damages (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 10(1))? No for life insurance or assurance paid on the death; superannuation, provident or like fund payments; friendly society, benefit society or trade union benefits; and pensions, benefits or allowances under the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986 or the Social Security Act 1991. The part of the estate passing to the family counts only so far as its value is above $250,000. The Act does not say for other payments.
GIVEN `what is the payment or benefit` IS A `Payment or benefit` @desc What it is: a sum under a contract of insurance or assurance paid or payable on the death; a sum from a superannuation, provident or like fund; a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union; a pension, benefit or allowance under the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986; a pension, benefit or allowance under the Social Security Act 1991; the part of the estate that passes to members of the family; or another payment or benefit
      `value of the estate passing to the family` IS A NUMBER @desc Only for the estate: the total value, in dollars, of the part of the estate of the person who died that passes to all the members of the family together (0 otherwise)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this payment be taken off our damages` MEANS
    `s 10(1) -- it may be taken into account in assessing damages` `what is the payment or benefit` `value of the estate passing to the family`

@export How much of the estate passing to my family can be taken into account against our damages for the death (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 10(1)(b))? Only the part of its value above $250,000; nothing if the value is $250,000 or less.
GIVEN `value of the estate passing to the family` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value, in dollars, of the part of the estate of the person who died that passes to all the members of the family together
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of the estate can be counted against our damages` MEANS
    `s 10(1)(b) -- the value that may be taken into account` `value of the estate passing to the family`

@export How much can our damages for the death include for this expense (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 10(2))? Funeral or cremation expenses in full; the reasonable cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave; and hospital and medical expenses incurred as a result of the wrongful act that caused the death -- each only if paid or incurred by the person bringing the action or a member of the family it is brought for, whether or not the family lost anything else. The Act does not say for other expenses.
GIVEN `what is the expense` IS AN `Expense` @desc The expense: funeral or cremation expenses; the cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave; hospital and medical expenses; or another expense
      `how much was it` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount incurred or paid, in dollars
      `was it paid or incurred by the plaintiff or a member of the family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was incurred or paid by the person bringing the action (the executor, administrator or a family member) or by a member of the family for whose benefit it is brought
      `did the hospital or medical expense result from the wrongful act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for hospital and medical expenses: whether they were incurred as a result of the wrongful act that caused the death
      `what is a reasonable cost for the headstone` IS A NUMBER @desc Only for a headstone or tombstone: the reasonable cost of erecting it, in dollars, as the court would find it (enter the amount paid if you think it reasonable)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much can our damages include for this expense` MEANS
    `s 10(2) -- the damages that may be awarded for the expense` `what is the expense` `how much was it` `was it paid or incurred by the plaintiff or a member of the family` `did the hospital or medical expense result from the wrongful act` `what is a reasonable cost for the headstone`

@export How much of the amount recovered in the action for the death do I receive (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 5)? The amount recovered, less the legal costs not recovered from the defendant, is divided among the family members in the shares the jury (or court) finds and directs. The costs are taken here in proportion to the shares.
GIVEN `the amount recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount recovered from the defendant in the action, in dollars
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action that were not recovered from the defendant, in dollars
      `my share as directed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the jury (or court) found and directed as your share, in dollars
      `all the shares as directed` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of the shares the jury (or court) found and directed for all the family members, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of the amount recovered do I receive` MEANS
    `s 5 -- my part of the amount recovered` `the amount recovered` `the costs not recovered from the defendant` `my share as directed` `all the shares as directed`

§§ `Money paid into court`
-- Arises only where the defendant has paid money into court ("Has this happened to you?").

@export If the defendant has paid money into court and we did not accept it, does the defendant win the issue of whether it was enough (Fatal Accidents Act 1934 (Tas) s 9)? The defendant may pay one sum for the whole family without dividing it. If it is not accepted, the plaintiff takes issue that it is not enough, and the jury thinks it is enough, the defendant is entitled to the verdict on that issue.
GIVEN `has money been paid into court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has paid money into court as compensation for the death
      `did we accept it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the money paid into court was accepted
      `did we take issue that it is not enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff took issue as to whether the sum is sufficient
      `does the jury think it is enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jury (or court) thinks the sum paid in is sufficient
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the defendant win the issue on the money paid into court` MEANS
    `s 9(2) -- the defendant is entitled to the verdict on that issue` `has money been paid into court` `did we accept it` `did we take issue that it is not enough` `does the jury think it is enough`
